A||||15||batboy||the Batmobile got spanked...blown away||||11||
Z||000000||batboy||10-19-2000||08:05 PM||||It was bound to happen. I headed home from work today and rolled up to the last stop light going out of town. This intersection is where the speed limit changes from 55 to 65 mph. I whipped an unsuspecting Plymouth Neon at this same stop light once.<P>The lead car in the lane next to me was a mean looking dark blue 1969 Firebird. It was all business. Ladder traction bars, skinny front drag tires, big meats in the back, looked like the rear wheel wells were tubbed, and a big ol' tach sat square in front of the driver on the dash. The throb of a big cube Pontiac motor drummed noisily, but sweetly through glass pack header mufflers, nearly drowning out the Batmobile.<P>A little devil appeared on my shoulder and whispered, "Let's see what he's got! Hehe!"<P>A little angel appeared on my other shoulder and looked crossly at the little red guy with the horns and forked tail, "Are you insane? Listen to that engine! Look at that classic Firebird! It will eat us alive!"<P>I listened. Yep, that was a wicked sounding lope. That rumpity-rump idle meant it had a big cam and was built to the max. It sounded awesome.<P>The devil slyly says, "Of course he'll take us on top end. I ain't stupid. Let's blow him away off the line and then shut down before he can get his rpm built up." I'm thinking... yeah, it just might work...and that would be cool.<P>The tiny fellow with the halo puts his hands on his hips and states, "Are we forgetting that the Batmobile's motor is tired and sick? What about that cylinder that's low on compression? You know, the same one that recently started fouling plugs and spewing out oil smoke."<P>Yeah, I know, I think to myself. The ol' gal has been burning oil bad lately and is sounding pretty raggity. Need a rebuild for sure. But... surely it's good for at least one more blast.<P>The little devil wrestles the angel down and duct tapes his mouth shut. The Devil looks up while he's sitting on the angel and grins. "Let's do it!"<P>All righty then! No cops! The road ahead is clear! The light turns green! I launch first, at my usual half throttle, and immediately get a nose on the ol' Firebird. Suddenly, I hear a thunderous roar of a 400 cube engine (6.6 liter) winding up to at least five grand and dropping the best and most impressive holeshot I've ever seen on the street. The 69 instantly exploded out to a car length lead.<P>HOLY GUANO! What happened? I see the nose of the Firebird dip and think: my only chance to make up some distance while he's shifting. I nail the Batmobile hard. The rear pulls to the left, but I'm expecting the torque steer and power on through it. I pull up to the rear tire of my noble opponant and then the 'bird finds it's way back into it's peak rpm range and begins quickly pulling away.<P>When the Firebird reaches a two car lead, the angel struggles free and kicks the devil right in the gonads. He pulls the duct tape off his mouth, straightens his halo, glares at me and yells, "Dork, I told you so!" I meekly shut down the embarressed Batmobile. A second later I see the 69 shut down too.<P>A few minutes later I pull up next to him as he slows down for traffic in his lane. We make brief eye contact and I give him a thumbs up. He seemed surprised at first, but then quickly grinned and gave me a nod.<P>Well...at least it was another Pontiac that humbled me. No more racing until the Batmobile gets it's new Z-28 roller motor next spring. That's the first race I ever lost that I didn't feel bad about. I was stupid, I knew better. That ol' Firebird was one a wicked SOB. I feel honored that I got to see it in action. Of course, no way he could hang with me in the curves... but, not a lot of those here in Kansas. Hmmmm.||||reg||11
Z||000001||Spyder||10-19-2000||09:08 PM||||Lol, I have to put myself in the other guys shoes:<BR>He thought that a plain old Fiero was giving him a hard time. He probably took his car home and had it on blocks to see what was wrong with his car.<BR>Batboy, you knew what you were against...couldn’t resist could ya.||||reg||7
Z||000002||My7Fieros||10-19-2000||10:17 PM||||MORE MORE!!! lol...man I felt like I was just reading a good book! Well, we all knew how the outcome would be, but it was a valiant effort..and like you said, at least it was another Pontiac.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||10-19-2000||10:31 PM||||That would be funny! He's on the classic Firebird Forum right now saying: I have a 69 with a totally built 400, pop-up forged pistons, tri-power, ram air IV heads, mega cam, big tube headers, and glass packs. What's the deal? I can only pull a two car lead on a Fiero. What are those guys doing to those 2.8 V6's nowadays? Maybe I'd better get nitrous.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ThatRickGuy||10-20-2000||11:23 AM||||&lt;shudder&gt; nitrous on a old pontiac engine? Pontiacs were long throws, huge torque low redline (like 5200 stock) awesome off the line but sucking for top end. I mean, you're driving a car with roughly the same areodynamics as a brick, things get a little unstable at 120... I'm super impressed by the Batmobile and your driving skills tho, beacuse with a good set up and a skilled driver, that old pontiac 400 will blow the doors off a lot of cars at the line. Man, I miss the Lemans at times like this  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tigger||10-20-2000||11:27 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by batboy:<BR><B>HOLY GUANO! What happened? I see the nose of the Firebird dip and think: my only chance to make up some distance while he's shifting. Hmmmm.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Lol, almost an excellent David and Goliath story...  Too bad you lost  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">, but I give you credit for trying to outrun the beast.<P><P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tigger.gif">           <BR>  <A HREF="mailto:RacingStripes@hotmail.com">RacingStripes@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||12
Z||000006||2M4 Dale||10-20-2000||11:35 AM||||Bat, after you get that new motor in you should go look the 'ol Firebird up for another go-round. Those Birds were fast but had a hellava time putting all those ponies on the ground.||||reg||14
Z||000007||Raydar||10-20-2000||06:04 PM||||Couple of months back, I was driving home from work and took my favorite twisty road. (the one I use to go around I-20 traffic.)<BR>As I was waiting to turn left from a 4-way, a *sweet* dark blue 69 Firebird, with the big 60 series tires and a <I>really healthy</I> rumble pulled across the intersection, going in the same direction that I was. I turned in behind him as he cleared the intersection. I hammered it at about the same time that he did.<BR>Welllll.... He just drove off and sucked the paint right of the front of my Formula! <BR>But it was fun to watch. Most folks that have old muscle cars fixed up as nice as this one usually don't drive them a whole lot. And stomp on it even less frequently. <BR>To be fair, he did look a bit unstable when he hit some of the curves that I can usually take flat-out. He had to slow to turn into a neighborhood after about 1/2 - 3/4 mile. <BR>I gave him a beep and a wave when I passed. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.||||reg||14
Z||000008||batboy||10-20-2000||08:46 PM||||Total proof that there's no replacement for displacement. When you have cubes to spare you can built up the top end hp and still have plenty of mid range torque. I used to have a 77 Trans Am with a 400, but I'd nearly forgot how impressive they are when built up.<P>Dale, that 69 Firebird didn't have much problem putting power to the ground. It was basically set up like a drag car. Narrowed rear axles, huge sticky rear tire, traction bars, and I'm guessing a locking rear diff too. It's been a long time since I heard an street engine that sounded so hyper and tight. I had a flashback to the 70's when muscle cars ruled and I had a big block Chevelle SS.||||reg||1
Z||000009||sparks||10-20-2000||09:46 PM||||Hmm, reminds me of when I had a black 68 Firebird.  Boy I miss that car.  It was a 350 HO w/ a Muncie 4 spd.  Fast and fun!  Decent in the curves.  Not sure how it compares to my Fiero tho, not many curves in Nebraska!  I miss that old Pontiac. That's why I just bought my Fiero Monday nite.  After 3 Firebird, 2 second gen and one first gen I bought a Jeep, then two others.  Still have two of 'em.<P>Still I missed the fun of the Pontiacs but my wife doesn't like the looks of the Birds but she doesn't mind the Fiero.  :^)<P>------------------<BR>--<BR>sparks||||reg||1
Z||000010||88formula||10-20-2000||10:01 PM||||Batboy, that story is funny as hell. You should be a writer. I think you have the most posts on this forum at 2898 posts, holly cow!||||reg||1
Z||000011||Pontiaddict||10-20-2000||11:01 PM||||Yeah, I love old Pontiacs too. It isn't hardly worth trying to find one in the rust belt though. I seen a nice 68 Lemans but they didn't have a price on it. (Couldn't afford it anyway.) I'd either find one of those, a 64 Catalina, or a 74-76 T/A.<P>Who needs to rev past 5200 when you have the torque to pull 2.77 gears?||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||10-21-2000||12:03 AM||||Monkeyman has lots more posts than me, he has over 4200 posts, I think.<P>Ah, the summer of '77, a buddy of mine had a nice 68 GTO with a custom chocolate brown and white paint job. He had money to burn, but not much engine building skills, so I helped him (or vice versa) put one powerful 400 together. Since I did so much work on it, he let me go cruising with it on the nights he had to work, sometimes. That ol' Goat was unbeatable on Nevada Avenue in Colorado Springs that year. It was an awesome car with a Muncie 4 speed "rock crusher" tranny and posi rear end. I can't believe we didn't get more tickets with that car than we did. It eventually got stolen and was later found totally stripped.||||reg||1
Z||000013||2M4 Dale||10-21-2000||12:46 AM||||I really like that old muscle too !!! My first car was a 67 GTO ragtop and my last true muscle car was a 69 GTO Judge. Only owned one Firebird and it was a 1979-301cid DOG !!!<P>Sounds like the one you raced was pretty serious ... lol||||reg||6
Z||000014||olympic||10-21-2000||08:34 PM||||  There are two muscle cars I will never forget, both were owned by 2 of my older brothers.  The first was a plain jane 77 Camaro that my brother dropped a '70 455 rocket into(~400 hp stock!!).  It was ported and polished, 3/4" lift cam, hirise intake, holley 4bbl, headers, header mufflers and zoomies(4 pipes out each side of the car)  The air cleaner would't fit under the hood so he cut a hole for it and glassed on a cowl induction scoop.  The rear tires were 14" wide and said "Formula 1" in white letters.  I still swear they were rain tires from an Indy car.  The best part was the full red on black crush velvet interior(hey, it was way cool in 1985!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  )  <P>  I remember when he used to let the car idle that it would visibly rock from side to side from the torque of the engine.  Raw fuel would spit from the exhaust.  I only rode in it a couple of times but that was enough!!  It could lay rubber for blocks and it would suck you back into the padded crush velvet seats all the way past 120 mph.  And my gawd, the sound!!  It was undescribable.  <P>  The second car was an AMC Javelin.  Black paint, black vinyl interior and a 401 with a 4 speed.  It was one mean looking car.  The previous owner had installed 4.56 gears which made the car a real monster.  It's top speed wasn't much over 100 mph but it could ripple the space-time continuum getting there  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>  15 years ago these kind of cars were as common as Civics and Stangs are today.  It's just sad that most of the old muscle cars are in storage or in the junk yards.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000015||Cheever2||10-21-2000||09:33 PM||||Ditto on missing the muscle days.  My brother had a 68 Chevelle SS 396, and he never lost a race.  Best I could afford was small blocks but still had fun in my Satellite Sebring.  Had a Lemans 350 for a little while.  <P>I too, was MOST impressed with Batboy's writing skills.  Could picture it in my mind.<BR>||||reg||1
